WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.960
When aiming, should your lower palm&nbsp;
be in contact with the mousepad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:10.140
or should it float? For the most amount of people,&nbsp;
you will aim better with contact, so long as it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.180
done correctly. The problem with floating is&nbsp;
that we sacrifice control, and in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.880
stopping power. Much like handwriting, we have&nbsp;
our hand on the desk to provide a stable base to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.980
move from. If you try to write with a floating&nbsp;
hand, it’s going to feel shaky and imprecise.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:31.500
The main benefit of aiming with a floating&nbsp;
lower palm is to reduce friction, so you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.480
move around the mousepad with less resistance.&nbsp;
The thing is, if that’s really a problem for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.160
there are much better solutions that don’t&nbsp;
require you to sacrifice your aiming ability.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.620
My personal favorites are wearing a sleeve, and&nbsp;
while this one doesn’t cover the lower palm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.760
the reduction in friction from the rest&nbsp;
of the arm can make up the difference.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.340
And frequently washing any skin that comes&nbsp;
in contact with the pad. This removes oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.120
sweat and clamminess, giving&nbsp;
you a much smoother guide.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:04.860
There are also sleeves that cover this area&nbsp;
like the skypad full sleeve if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.540
go down that route. Just keep in mind that it can&nbsp;
make gripping your mouse a little more difficult.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:15.720
So what’s so good about having it on the desk?&nbsp;
Firstly, it provides a stable base for your wrist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.820
and fingers to aim from. This doesn’t mean you&nbsp;
just plant it to one spot and aim from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.500
it should stay loose and move around with&nbsp;
you. The main perk of this stable base,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:30.120
is actually friction. Now friction&nbsp;
can get a bad wrap in aiming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.100
and people go to all sorts of lengths to try&nbsp;
and minimize it, but some amount is crucial.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:41.460
This is most evident with flick shots and stopping&nbsp;
power. Stopping power being the ability to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.920
moving when you want to, like when your crosshair&nbsp;
is over a target. Having this contact means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.780
when you stop putting effort into a movement,&nbsp;
your mouse will stop almost immediately due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:57.360
to that friction. Without it, stopping primarily&nbsp;
comes down to your muscles stabilizing the mouse.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:02.280
You can try this yourself actually. Just be&nbsp;
careful not to throw your mouse across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.620
room. Do a fast flick with the gap and you&nbsp;
will feel a wobble left and right in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:13.440
hand and arm as it stabilizes. Then do the&nbsp;
same with no gap, and you may still feel a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:18.300
wobble, but it will be much weaker as the&nbsp;
friction is helping to put on the brakes.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.700
To use contact optimally, the feet of your&nbsp;
mouse should be in-line with your lower palm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.880
and the skin of your thumb&nbsp;
should be a tiny bit lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:32.280
simply because it flattens out a little when&nbsp;
it’s on the desk. The best way to ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:36.780
your mouse grip isn’t causing problems with&nbsp;
this, is to relax your hand on the mousepad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:41.760
fingers curled as if you were holding your&nbsp;
mouse. You can swipe around a little, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.380
contact you feel on the lower palm is the same&nbsp;
as what you should feel when holding the mouse.&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:52.740
The mouse should be able to fit into hand&nbsp;
without raising up the lower palm. When we grip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:58.500
this pocket in our hand opens up, and that’s where&nbsp;
our mouse sits, we don’t want it sitting up here.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.480
Now that we know what the lower palm contact&nbsp;
feels like, i’d like to share a tip for it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.880
that I find extremely useful to my aim. You&nbsp;
don’t need the full weight of your hand on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:13.980
the mousepad. What we can do, is slightly&nbsp;
lift up our hand. This doesn’t mean there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.940
should be any gap, but the sensation&nbsp;
is like taking weight off the hand.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:24.300
This is awesome, because it lets us dynamically&nbsp;
adjust our own friction. Unlike other techniques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.920
like fingertip skating that i’ll link to in&nbsp;
the description, while it is dynamic, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:34.500
don’t really need to keep changing it on the fly.&nbsp;
Mess around with how much weight you take off, to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:39.420
find something that requires little to no effort&nbsp;
to maintain, that takes off just enough friction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.760
to make moving around your mousepad feel nice and&nbsp;
crisp. You don’t need to go overboard with this.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.260
Without it, if you’re totally relaxed with&nbsp;
the full weight of your hand on the desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:53.100
that friction can make your aim&nbsp;
feel muddy, slow and unresponsive.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.600
Now I know there are some outliers&nbsp;
like Twistzz who aim with a gap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.500
but for the vast majority of&nbsp;
people, no gap performs the best.

